<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Accra</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Accra is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea">Gulf of Guinea</a>. It grew from towns of the Ga people, and in the 17th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> built forts there: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ussher-fort">Ussher Fort</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-fort">James Fort</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osu-castle">Christiansborg</a>. It became the capital of the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> colony in 1877 and of independent Ghana in 1957.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/accra.jpg" alt="Accra" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Amoat7 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Acca.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Accra lies on the southern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea">Gulf of Guinea</a>. The name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> word Nkran, meaning ants, a reference to the soldier ants and the anthills of the countryside around it. In the Ga language the town is called Ga, the same name as the Ga people. The historian Carl Christian Reindorf proposed a link between the martial qualities and migratory behaviour of the ants and those of the Ga. Anthills were sacred places, seen as points of contact between the known world and the world of the dead. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> spelled the name Akra.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By the 15th century the kingdom of Accra ruled the area from its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayawaso">Ayawaso</a>. Local inhabitants destroyed a Portuguese fort in 1576. In the 17th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> built Fort Crèvecœur, later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ussher-fort">Ussher Fort</a>, and the British built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-fort">James Fort</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osu-castle">Christiansborg</a> also stood there. In 1677 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akwamu">Akwamu</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ansa-sasraku">Ansa Sasraku</a> sacked Ayawaso and beheaded the king, Okai Koi. The king's son Ofori retreated to Small Accra, the town beneath Fort Crèvecœur, and was driven into exile in Fetu in 1681.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> gradually acquired the other powers' interests, beginning in 1851 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> sold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osu-castle">Christiansborg</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> was the last to sell, in 1871. In 1877, at the end of the second Anglo-Asante War, Accra replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-coast">Cape Coast</a> as capital of the British Gold Coast, because its climate was drier. After a bubonic plague epidemic in 1908 the native-only neighbourhood of Adabraka was created to relieve crowding. A bridge over the Korle Lagoon in 1923 spurred settlement to the west, and cocoa became the largest export.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Accra is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, and the Greater Accra Region has about 5.46 million people. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a>, Ghana's first Prime Minister from 1957, drew up his own plan for the city. Its architecture ranges from 19th-century colonial buildings to modern towers. The Parliament, the Supreme Court and Black Star Square are there, and the city has an international airport and railway links to Tema, Sekondi-Takoradi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Accra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Fante and the Ga of the new town of <b>Accra</b> looked to the British for protection, and in 1821 the Crown dissolved the company and gave the forts to Charles MacCarthy, governor of Sierra Leone, with a mandate to impose peace and end the slave trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast Colony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…destroyed the state: Doe was captured and killed in 1990, rival factions controlled much of the country, and Taylor, elected president in 1997, faced a renewed insurgency until he resigned and went into exile in Nigeria in August 2003, a week before the <b>Accra</b> peace agreement was signed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liberia">Liberia · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Accra</b> hosted the first Conference of Independent African States in April 1958 and the All-African People’s Conference that December, attended by nationalist leaders such as Patrice Lumumba.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>In Ghana, Nkrumah began infrastructure and economic plans after independence in 1957, including $16 million for a new town at Tema as a seaport for <b>Accra</b>, and thousands of new rural schools.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/african-socialism">African socialism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>Akwamu was founded by Akan migrants in the hills north of <b>Accra</b>, with its capital at Nyanaoase.</blockquote><a href="/state/akwamu">Akwamu</a></li><li><blockquote>Denmark built Fort Frederiksborg near Cape Coast and in 1661 bought the site of Christiansborg at Osu, near modern <b>Accra</b>, from the king of Accra.</blockquote><a href="/state/danish-gold-coast">Danish Gold Coast</a></li></ul></section>
